# MinerTim CLI configuration
# Copy to mining.conf and fill in your values:
#   cp mining.conf.example mining.conf

# NOTE: as surveyed 2026-09-13 this pool presents a self-signed certificate
# (CN=mining.pool), so it needs TLS_FINGERPRINT set — see the bottom of this
# file. A pool with an ordinary certificate needs no extra configuration.
POOL=pool.supportxmr.com:443
WALLET=YOUR_WALLET_ADDRESS_HERE
# THREADS: leave unset to auto-detect performance cores.
THREADS=
# DONATE_LEVEL: percent of mining time donated (default 5, minimum 1), split
# 50/50 between the MinerTim author and XMRig. See README.
DONATE_LEVEL=5
# NATIVE_LOOP: "on" (default) or "off". Fallback switch for the native-loop JIT,
# which emits the whole 2048-iteration RandomX loop as ARM64 and is worth roughly
# 7% hashrate (aarch64, rx/0, full mode only — ignored elsewhere).
#
# Turn it OFF if the pool starts rejecting shares. A JIT defect does not crash
# the miner: it produces a wrong-but-plausible hash, so the hashrate still looks
# healthy and the only symptom is rejects climbing on the pool dashboard. This
# switch lets you rule the JIT in or out by restarting instead of rebuilding.
# Equivalent to `--native-loop off`, or MINERTIM_NATIVE_LOOP=0 in the environment.
NATIVE_LOOP=
# VERIFY_SHARES: "on" (default) or "off". Before submitting a share, recompute it
# with the older, well-tested code path and compare. If they disagree the share is
# withheld and a loud error is logged, so the fault is caught here instead of
# showing up days later as rejected shares on the pool.
#
# What it does and does not cover: both paths share the same instruction
# generator, so this detects faults in the NEW native-loop machinery (which is
# where the recent changes are) and would not detect a fault in code common to
# both. It is a targeted check, not a general guarantee.
#
# Costs about 0.005% of mining time: only the rare hashes good enough to submit
# are re-checked, not the hundreds per second that are discarded. Leave it on.
VERIFY_SHARES=
# TLS_FINGERPRINT: pin the pool's TLS certificate by its SHA-256 fingerprint,
# 64 hex characters. Colons are fine — paste openssl's output as-is.
#
# Leave this UNSET unless you need it. By default MinerTim fully verifies the
# pool's certificate: trust chain, hostname and expiry. That is what you want,
# and it needs no configuration.
#
# You need a pin when a pool's certificate cannot pass that check. Several
# Monero pools ship the stock self-signed certificate that comes with the pool
# daemon software — it is named CN=mining.pool or CN=mining.proxy, so it fails
# on both trust and hostname no matter how long it is valid for. Surveyed
# 2026-09-13, pool.supportxmr.com:443 and gulf.moneroocean.stream:20128 are both
# in this category.
#
# A pin is not a bypass. It says "accept exactly this certificate", so if someone
# substitutes their own, the connection fails — which is precisely what accepting
# everything cannot detect.
#
# Read a pool's fingerprint:
#
#   openssl s_client -connect <host>:<port> -servername <host> </dev/null 2>/dev/null \
#     | openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256
#
# Two things to know before you rely on it:
#
#   * You are trusting whatever certificate you saw the first time. If you read
#     it over a connection someone is already intercepting, you have pinned the
#     attacker. Read it from a network you trust, ideally more than one.
#   * A pin breaks when the pool renews its certificate, and mining stops until
#     you update this line. Pools using Let's Encrypt rotate roughly every 90
#     days; the self-signed pool certificates above are typically issued for a
#     century and never change.
TLS_FINGERPRINT=
